# Physics compute strategy

Status: proposal, informed by local measurements on 2026-09-10.

Use CPU collision queries as the current default and fallback. Preserve a path to
validated CPU/GPU selection; a single Apple M5 measurement does not justify a
permanently CPU-only architecture. Whether the M5 leads a particular single-thread
ranking is not established by this experiment and is unnecessary to this decision.

## What the benchmark establishes

Five implementations queried identical finite rays against 32,768 triangles in
an isolated terminal-controlled headless Chromium process. Chromium diagnostics
confirmed the actual Apple M5 Metal GPU; WebGPU reported a non-fallback adapter.
All four prototypes matched the production picker across 62,532 rays.

Median milliseconds per entire nearby-hit batch:

| Rays | Production CPU | CPU linear scan | CPU BVH | GPU linear scan | GPU BVH |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 5 | 2.25 | 0.500 | 0.000806 | 9.0 | 0.4 |
| 1,024 | 465.8 | 98.3 | 0.2125 | 17.8 | 0.5 |

GPU values include upload, dispatch and results returned to CPU memory. Short CPU
values use calibrated repeated batches and are hot-cache averages. These are
query timings, not game FPS, CPU utilization or energy measurements. The GPU
linear scan assigns one invocation per ray; it does not test every possible
parallel reduction or traversal implementation.

CPU BVH's advantage over GPU BVH was about 497 times for five rays but only
2.35 times for 1,024 rays. Another device or implementation could move the
crossover. GPU linear scanning already beat CPU linear scanning at 1,024 rays;
the algorithm matters as well as the processor.

The current aircraft generates up to five body rays per 120 Hz step. Steps depend
on preceding collision results, so combining many steps into a large batch would
change the simulation. CPU access to WebGPU results awaits completion through
[buffer mapping](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/GPUBuffer/mapAsync).
That dependency remains relevant across hardware, but its cost must be measured.

The [complete benchmark table and methodology](../../benchmarks/collision/README.md)
includes distant misses, preparation costs and reproducible terminal commands.
[Raw results](../../benchmarks/collision/results-gpu.json) include samples,
