## What does not generalize yet

This is one CPU, GPU, memory system and browser version with no concurrent game
rendering or tile streaming. Older CPUs, mobile devices, integrated GPUs, discrete
GPUs, browser drivers, cache pressure and thermal limits can change both paths.
A faster GPU does not by itself predict lower end-to-end small-batch latency.
Artificially throttling this M5 would be a sensitivity test, not a substitute for
real devices. Power and battery efficiency need separate measurements.

The promising general direction is to avoid unnecessary triangle tests. Direct
raster height lookup is already specialized for terrain. Dense arbitrary meshes
are candidates for a spatial index/BVH. A CPU-built tree cost 60.7 ms in this
fixture, so worker construction, streaming updates, memory and disposal matter
alongside steady query speed. Production BVH integration is future work.

## Proposed implementation sequence

1. Keep the existing finite-ray CPU backend and the corrected passive collision
   response. Separate contact queries from JSBSim's CPU/WASM dynamics solver.
2. Integrate a production CPU spatial index for dense static tiles behind a clear
   query contract. Preserve nearest-hit, finite-segment, transform, normal and
   visible-tile semantics; measure construction, replacement and disposal costs.
3. Test a production GPU candidate on representative devices and normal game
   loads. Geometry must remain resident, results must carry terrain generation
   and aircraft-state identity, and late/stale results must never drive a newer
   physics step. Device loss or unavailable support must safely use the CPU.
4. Expose Auto / CPU / GPU once both production paths are correct. Auto should
   measure actual batch latency including CPU readback, p95 delay and preparation
   cost; switch only for a sustained meaningful benefit. Show the active backend,
   support an explicit user override, and report any necessary fallback.

No gameplay CPU/GPU selector or GPU dynamics solver is implemented by this work.
The GPU kernels and CPU BVH remain benchmark prototypes. CPU-only operation today
is the available production path, not a permanent hardware policy.

## Crash response already implemented

Body impacts now select the earliest swept probe, preserve its offset from the
centre, account for attitude, normalize the contact normal and use dissipative
restitution by default. Deep ground penetration stops integration before loading
unbounded springs, using the seven actual C172 contacts and current centre of
gravity. Clear banked and pitched flight must keep running.

Settings → Ground impacts → Arcade ground launches restores exaggerated launches
as an opt-in persistent mode. The default hard-impact stop is not a complete
crash/damage simulation. See the
[crash investigation and regression evidence](../../../flight-sim/docs/near-ground-performance.md#crash-response-follow-up).

Flight-sim validation passed lint, all 375 tests and a production build. Tests
include high-speed impacts, ordinary touchdowns, terrain revisions, engine-state
restoration, banked clearance and the optional arcade behavior. Benchmark runs
use terminal/headless automation without controlling the user's cursor; this
preference is recorded in both repositories' AGENTS.md files.
